    "explanation": "This dispute is decided on the trip's own trace and fare record, and both of you should read the reasoning the same way.\n\n1. Was the route a deviation? Yes, on the measurement. The GPS polyline (EV-GPS-00103, 100% coverage, 175 fixes, no gaps, max gap 0 s) records 11.18 km against the booked optimal of 8.2 km (EV-TRIP-00103) — an excess of 2.98 km, or 36.34%. That clears both POL-RD-01 gates (> 0.8 km and > 20%), and the trace figure governs over the rounded 11.2 km convenience copy. The excess is below the 50% / 8.0 km materiality lines, so this is not a MATERIAL deviation and POL-RD-06 is not engaged.\n\n2. Is the deviation excused? Yes, entirely. LTA advisory ADV-20260714-031 (EV-ADVIS-00103-01) closes Braddell Road (towards Serangoon) between Lorong 1 Toa Payoh and Bishan Rd — precisely the arterial the platform's own optimal route uses ('Braddell Rd -> Serangoon Rd -> Kallang arterials', optimal_route_erp_sgd 0.00). Its window 05:30–12:00 overlaps the trip (08:07–08:36), the route leaves the corridor before the closed segment and rejoins after, and all three POL-RD-03 limbs are satisfied. The exoneration cap is min(2.98, 1.30 x (10.6 - 8.2) + 1.00) = min(2.98, 4.12) = 2.98 km, so the whole excess is justified. Because nothing remains after the adjustment, POL-RD-02 has no residual distance or time to refund: base fare 3.80, distance fare 8.40 and time fare 8.12 stand as charged, and no POL-RD-02 figure is added.\n\n3. The ERP line, which is the part of this bill the rider is right about. The fare breakdown (EV-FARE-00103) charges two ERP gantries — CTE (Braddell Rd towards City) at 3.00 and CTE (Moulmein Rd towards City) at 1.00 — and records on_optimal_route false for both, and the trace passes them at 0.0 m and 40.2 m respectively, well inside the 250 m crossing test (EV-GPS-00103). They are therefore real crossings of a corridor the rider's booked route never used. POL-RD-09(2) provides for exactly this situation: where the diversion is excused as an external event, distance and time charges stand because the driver is not at fault, but off-route ERP is refunded to the rider as a standalone pass-through correction, because the rider did not cause the closure either. That is 3.00 + 1.00 = 4.00, inside the 12.00 per-trip cap. POL-RD-09(1) does not double-count it, because there is no POL-RD-02 refund for it to be added on top of.\n\n4. Points raised and answered. The driver's chat message at 08:12:30 ('braddell close today, i take CTE ah'), sent before the first gantry at 08:14:06, is contemporaneous notice of the diversion, not a rider instruction — the rider's 'ok' is a bare acknowledgement, waypoint_hint is null on both messages, and no rider-requested detour under POL-RD-04 exists, so POL-RD-09(3) does not bar the ERP correction. No POL-RD-06 conduct referral is produced: the deviation is not MATERIAL and the driver's profile shows deviation_flags_90d of 0 against 553 completed trips in 90 days, clear of both pattern tests. No billing-integrity referral under POL-RD-09(4) arises either, because both gantry lines are matched to the trace rather than being phantom lines. Every artifact relied on here carries weight_multiplier 1.0 and no issue codes, so no discount applies and none had to be disclosed.\n\nResult: of the 9.00 contested, the distance and time elements stand and 4.00 of off-route ERP is returned to the rider. The charge is therefore partially refunded.",
