{"ruling":{"ruling":"PARTIAL_REFUND","amount_sgd":4.0,"driver_compensation_sgd":null,"referrals":[],"confidence":0.9,"explanation":"Both of you agree on the shape of this trip, and the evidence supports that agreement. The GPS trace (EV-GPS-00103) records 11.18 km against the booked optimal of 8.2 km — an excess of 2.98 km, or 36.34% — which clears both POL-RD-01 gates (greater than 0.8 km and greater than 20% of optimal), so a route deviation is established. The LTA advisory (EV-ADVIS-00103-01) names Braddell Road (towards Serangoon) between Lorong 1 Toa Payoh and Bishan Rd — the arterial the platform's own optimal route ('Braddell Rd -> Serangoon Rd -> Kallang arterials') traverses — its window (05:30–12:00) covers the trip, and the trace shows the vehicle leaving the optimal corridor before the closed segment and rejoining after. POL-RD-03 therefore exonerates the diversion: for the driver, the closure was real and the detour was driven because of it, not opportunistically (the driver told the rider at 08:12:30, before the first gantry at 08:14:06). The exoneration cap is min(2.98, 1.30 × (10.6 − 8.2) + 1.00) = min(2.98, 4.12) = 2.98 km, which absorbs the whole excess. No residual excess remains, so no distance or time refund runs under POL-RD-02 and the meter lines (base $3.80, distance $8.40, time $8.12) stand. That answers the driver's first four points in the driver's favour.

The one correction the policy requires is the ERP, and here the rider is right. The booked route carried no gantries at all (trip_record.optimal_route_erp_sgd = 0.00), and both CTE gantry lines on the bill — $3.00 at 08:14:06 and $1.00 at 08:19:41 — are marked on_optimal_route false and are matched by the trace within the 250 m crossing test (nearest fixes 0.0 m and 40.2 m). Because the diversion is an external event under POL-RD-03, POL-RD-09(2) leaves the distance and time charges standing but refunds the off-route ERP as a standalone pass-through correction, capped at $12.00; at $4.00 it is well inside the cap. POL-RD-09(3) does not bar the refund: the only message proposing the CTE came from the driver, the rider's 'ok' is a bare acknowledgement rather than an instruction, and with waypoint_hint null on both messages the rider-instruction test in POL-RD-04 cannot be met. Under POL-RD-09(1) this $4.00 is not added on top of any distance refund — it is the whole of the correction. The rider's request is therefore granted as to the ERP and refused as to anything beyond it.

No further money moves. The deviation is not material (2.98 km sits below both the 50%-of-optimal and the 8.0 km lines) and driver_profile.deviation_flags_90d is 0 against 553 completed trips in 90 days, so no POL-RD-06 uplift and no driver-conduct referral arises. The trace is unbroken — 175 fixes at a 10-second interval, 100% coverage, no gaps — so it is not impaired under POL-RD-07 and there is no gap-based escalation under POL-RD-10(3). Both gantry lines are matched to the trace, so there is no billing-integrity referral under POL-RD-09(4). The trip identifiers match across trip_record, gps_trace and fare_breakdown, and the timestamps are consistent, so no POL-RD-10 trigger fires. All artifacts relied on carry weight_multiplier 1.0, so the confidence cap is 1.0. In short: the detour is excused, the distance and time fares stand, and $4.00 of off-route ERP is returned to the rider."},"discarded_claims":[]}

